unities  throughout the nation mr. McKinzie  can tell you about that and about some  of the other efforts that are being made  to find out what's in the air what  happens to it and how it can be better  controlled you actually sample area mr.  macKensie yes we are sampling the air  because before we can effectively keep  pollutants out of the air we have to  find out precisely what's in the air  consequently sampling stations have been  set up in all the states and territories  in about a hundred and twelve urban and  49 non urban sites they are operated by  volunteer agencies usually local health  departments using equipment furnished by  the Public Health Service dusts there  and non gaseous pollutants in the open  air are collected at these stations and  sent to our laboratories in Cincinnati  they're the sample is analyzed and at  3-month intervals the results of  analyses are given to the sampling  agencies for sampling suspended solids  this apparatus which is similar to the  conventional household vacuum cleaner  sucks a measured volume of air through a  filter over a 24-hour period  each of these samplers filter about  70,000 cubic feet of air through a sheet  of paper made of glass fibers liquid  droplets are filtered also and retained  along with the solid particles well why  don't the pollutants just blow away they  do sometimes Carl but whether or not the  pollutants blow away depends entirely on  the wind and other movements of the air  normally on a nice clear day the air  near the ground is warmer than that  higher up and less tends to rise and  carry the pollutants up with it  sometimes however the higher air is  hotter than that at the ground because  of a particular weather condition called  an inversion at such times the air near  the ground cannot rise and the  pollutants tend to accumulate just as  though there were a lid over the entire  area  inversion situations may persist for  several days has occurred this fall in  Los Angeles causing several smog alerts  people now people often get the idea  that an area must be surrounded by hills  or mountains which form a kind of  natural bowl for this inversion lid to  cover while a natural bowl formation  helps keep the pollutants close to the  ground  we don't need Hills or valleys around a  given area in order to have an inversion  an inversion can occur over a town  situated on the plains now that we're  learning more and more about what causes  pollution communities are better able to  deal with it let's find out what some of  them are doing about it let's go first  to Arnold arch a very important man in  air pollution control work he is  president of the Air Pollution Control  Association and director of the air  pollution control division for Niagara  Falls what do you have to tell us  the city of Niagara Falls New York has  long been known by the world as being  famous for its cataracts and as a resort  area for honeymooners Niagara Falls is  far more important as one of the most  heavily concentrated and diversified  industrial areas in the world the fame  of our industries is known the world  over it is not surprising that our air  pollution problems caused the city of  Niagara Falls to adopt the first  all-inclusive air pollution control  ordinance in New York State cooperation  will industry is the basic theme of our  program and it has certainly paid off  thanks Arnold and now to give us an  insight of the air pollution situation  in Detroit let's switch to Morton  sterling Air Pollution Control Officer  for the Motor City hello Carl  Shirley right in the middle of our new  multi-million dollar Civic Center as you  can guess there's going to be a lot of  white marble buildings located here and  we hope to keep them that way although  you can't see it in the scene right now  Windsor Canada is right across from us  across the Detroit River the  International aspects of our air  pollution problem are obvious and I  believe unique in this country we hope  to solve our transient ship smoke  problem through the International Joint  Commission this year and in Cincinnati  Charles Gruber is standing by to explain  their particular situation Cincinnati  has made excellent progress in dealing  with sources of visible air pollution  today we have 90 percent fewer smoking  chimneys than ten years ago  Cincinnati industries have also made  good progress in combating fly ash dust  fumes and odors we must see that our  community is informed and motivated to  action as technical progress points the  way to cleaner  fear outdoor air well let's pick up  another southern city again this time  we'll hear from Jacksonville Florida  where HD Peters is the division the head  of the director of the division of  sanitation and TB art is Assistant  sanitary engineer come in Jacksonville  yes chorim who injects people are  conscious of air pollution problems and  while a lot of the cute as in no highly  industrialized cities there do require  attention fortunately we know our  trouble spots and the good cooperation  are making progress towards control  orders are more concerned than smoke and  present more Venusian problem than  anything else  of course as such they should be kept to  a minimum  now let's sweep clear up to San  Francisco where Ben Winsky air pollution  control officer will tell us about their  novel arrangement under which six  counties and seventy communities are  cooperating to combat air pollution we  are standing here on quite tower on  telegraph hill in San Francisco I am  Benjamin Lenski air pollution control  officer in the San Francisco Bay Area  where we have recently started our  scientifically based air pollution  reduction and control program because  air pollution flows across political  boundaries we have merged six county  lines and seventy city limits into a  special government Control District no  other large community anywhere in the  world has ever yet has as clean an  atmosphere as its people really 